## Field Requirements
- `appearance`: Age range, physique, clothing, distinctive features, 1-2 sentences; when no description in text, reasonably infer based on character type.
- `personality`: 2-3 core personality traits, 1-2 sentences; must be extracted from dialogue, actions, decisions.
- `backstory`: Background story and motivations, 2-3 sentences; explain personality causes combining profession, experiences, desires, fears, or secrets.
- `arc`: Expected character change trajectory, 1-2 sentences; clearly state "current gap → pressure source → possible change"; minor characters can leave as empty string.
- `relationships`: New relationships inferable from text, format as `{"character name": "relationship description"}`; only include new relationships not yet appearing in existing relationship list; if no inferable new relationships, output empty object `{}`.
- `voice`: Character's voice/language characteristic description, 1-2 sentences; extract from dialogue style, vocabulary habits, tone characteristics; if no dialogue in text, reasonably infer but must note inference basis.
- `gender`: Can only output `male`, `female`, `unknown`, or empty string; if existing information is insufficient, prefer empty string.
- `social_status`: Only supplement text-evident profession, title, class, identity changes; do not use placeholders like "ordinary/unknown".
- `abilities`: Only supplement text-evident stable abilities, resources, or professional skills; leave empty string when no new information.
- Infer based on text content, do not fabricate information contradicting the text; if unable to infer, can reasonably supplement but must align with character type.
- `appearance`, `personality`, `backstory`, `arc`, `voice` must be single-paragraph strings; prohibit splitting into objects, arrays, or structures with sub-fields; `social_status`, `abilities` must also remain single-paragraph strings.

## Pre-Output Self-Check
Silently check before generating final results:
- Can each inference connect back to words, actions, circumstances in the chapter, character type, or existing relationships?
- Are stable identity fields not writing weak inferences as definite facts?
- `relationships` only contains new relationships or relationship upgrades, not repeating existing relationship list?
- Are all text fields single-paragraph strings without nested objects, arrays, or placeholder words?